WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:08.750
Hello, I’m Warren Robins of MediDent FS,
I’m an income protection specialist for

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.960
dentists and medical doctors.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.120
Todays topic is, Who offers the best income
protection for dentists?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.790
The answer to that question is, not surprisingly,
it depends on your specific needs

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.650
but there are some general guidelines that
can save you a lot of money,

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:31.740
when you’re choosing income protection,
so if you already have a policy or if, quite

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.730
sensibly you’re looking to take it out,
keep watching,

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:39.750
as spending a couple of minutes here could
seriously reduce your future spending on income

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:40.750
protection.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:48.020
As a dentist, there are a lot of people competing
for your business and this is certainly the

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.760
case with income protection.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.780
The most popular insurers in dentistry are
Dentists Provident and Wesleyan, both of which

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.109
heavily trumpet the benefits you get from
their expertise in the dental and medical

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:03.219
fields.

00:01:03.219 --> 00:01:07.470
While I would totally agree that a detailed
knowledge of the dental field is a valuable

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:13.240
asset when advising on dentists income protection,
that knowledge is also available through independent

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.540
brokers specialising in dentists,
like MediDent FS,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.270
with the added advantage that they don't offer
policies from just one insurance company.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:27.979
If you want to get an unbiased analysis of
how Dentists Provident or Wesleyan policies

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:33.049
compare to other insurers,
you obviously won't get that from their advisors.

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:38.790
You can expect both to tell you that their
policy is the best for dentists but realistically,

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.200
what else are they going to say?

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:47.460
A dental specialist independent adviser however,
is not tied to one insurance company,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.369
so can offer unbiased advice on all the options
that are available

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:58.000
and they can almost certainly also offer significant
savings in both the monthly premium and the

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.020
full policy term cost.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.180
If you really want the best policy, it makes
sense to speak to Wesleyan and / or Dentist

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.189
Provident
but then make sure

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:11.200
that you also speak to a specialist independent
broker as well.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.650
That way you'll be making a fully informed
decision on who to take your Dentist income

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.480
protection with
and considering how long these policies run

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.740
for,
it’s a decision you really don’t want

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:26.840
to get wrong because if you do
it could easily cost you an extra £40k, even

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.980
for a modest IP policy.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.160
I know that sounds crazy but that’s the
reality.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:38.230
As long as you are comparing income protection
policies offering Own Occupation Cover, which

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.790
pays out if you can’t work as a dentist,
the core benefit is essentially the same with

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:47.349
any insurer,
dental specialist or not, as they are all

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:50.560
regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.060
This means that, unless you have specific
factors, such as working through your own

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.540
Ltd Company,
you can look for the best value policy available

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.650
but, you’ll be surprised
to hear, this doesn’t

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:06.349
always mean the lowest starting premium
and I’ll explain why.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:11.010
The reason is, when insurers apply the annual
cover increases, the premium goes up by more

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.150
than the cover, with the exception of one
insurer

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.480
and there are more than 10 different increase
methods used by insurers

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:24.790
and here are some examples .
The result is that for any given cover increase,

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.340
there will be quite a range of different premium
increases.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.120
Finding out which method is used by an insurer
is really difficult, unless you talk to me,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.000
because this information is buried deep in
the policy documents, which you’re only

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.640
given AFTER you’ve taken a policy out.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.370
The best way to illustrate this is to show
you what happens to the premiums of different

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.700
insurers with the same amount of annual cover
increase,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.489
if they all started at the same initial premium.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:56.620
Obviously they don’t start at the same cost
in reality but this does show the impact of

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.709
the different increase method on the long
term cost.

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:05.780
As you can see here, the age costed increase
method, used by both Wesleyan and Dentists

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:09.170
Provident
becomes increasingly expensive as you get

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:12.579
older, leading to a much bigger full term
cost,

00:04:12.579 --> 00:04:18.019
compared to most other increase methods apart
from Vitality but that's only half the story.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.209
A number of insurers use the same increase
method, so Zurich, Aviva and LV all follow

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:20.209
the L&amp;G line.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:23.229
If you apply the different increase methods
to real premiums, it makes interesting reading

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.040
for dentists.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.139
This projection shows total costs as follows.

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:35.599
Holloway at just under £29k, Dentist Provident
at just under £69k and Wesleyan at over £85k,

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:37.310
so massive cost differences.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.660
In theory, you can see the cost impact I’ve
shown here, on insurer quotation documents,

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:46.419
as they have to include the full term cost
but here’s the thing.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:50.419
Insurers write in disclaimers, so the total
costs they show assume the starting premium

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:54.680
does this…………it never changes, so,
completely unrealistic.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.900
A realistic cost, if the annual cost of living
increases at the same level as the last 35

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:03.000
years, just 3.6%,
can be between 2 and 6 times what’s shown

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.450
on the quote, depending on the insurer.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.980
These disclaimers are subtle, as you can see,
where I’ve highlighted the one used by Dentists

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.380
Provident
but what they hide is pretty shocking.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.470
The premium differences shown in my example
are not unusual.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:23.050
Wesleyan and Dentist Provident starting premiums
are normally quite a bit more expensive than

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:30.910
some other insurance companies, which only
exaggerates the age costed increase method.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:35.069
Using data from dentists I've spoken to over
the last 3 to 4 years, Dentists Provident

00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:40.740
averages around 40% more expensive, and Wesleyan
averages around 55% more expensive, versus

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.990
the best available starting premium.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:48.270
When you can work out realistic overall costs,
what you see is that certain insurers are

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.080
normally very expensive, such as Wesleyan
&amp; Dentists Provident,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.740
Vitality, Cirencester Friendly and British
Friendly Society,

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.120
so unless you’re happy to overpay, you can
normally rule them out

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.050
but there are plenty of other insurers to
choose from,

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.680
all offering equivalent cover to that offered
by dental specialist insurers.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.140
OK, if you speak to an independent insurance
adviser, which insurance company are they

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.340
going to recommend?

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:19.400
Well, that depends entirely on your individual
circumstances,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:24.259
are you a sole trader or do you work through
a Ltd company for instance,

00:06:24.259 --> 00:06:29.830
so in reality there is no single insurer that’s
the best choice for every dentist.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:35.370
Also, how the insurance is set up is equally
important and insurers vary

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.800
in their competitiveness
for different cover arrangements and this

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:43.740
also changes over time,
so there are many variables.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.050
The key point is to get expert independent
advice, from an adviser

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.759
that can forecast and compare realistic full
term costs

00:06:51.759 --> 00:06:55.240
and that is what we pride ourselves on at
MediDent FS.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:59.070
Oh, and by the way, a word of warning.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:03.760
There are two types of independent insurance
broker available, regarding the premiums that

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.800
they charge.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.180
The first type charges exactly the same premium
as you would pay going direct to the insurance

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:12.919
company, such as MediDent FS.

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:18.550
The second type receives a boosted commission
amount, which is paid for by increasing the

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:22.660
monthly premiums,
typically by 10 to 20%, which will cost you

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.050
an enormous amount extra over the policy term.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.710
Just ask a broker if their premiums are loaded
and if they are,

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.570
I suggest you look elsewhere.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:38.720
Thanks for watching, I’m Warren Robins,
the income protection specialist for Dentists

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:39.320
&amp; doctors.

